  "body": "The maintainer reported this in chat on 2026-11-14:\n\n> on the WCS page - under Quarter turns - there should be a \"flip X\", \"flip Y\" and \"flip Z\" button.. and what that does is flipping X+ to X- direction and vice versa for the other direction - without turning the gizmo around nother axis. As some machine has their directions reversed...\n\nExpected: three buttons, `Flip X`, `Flip Y` and `Flip Z`, are below Quarter turns on the WCS page. Each button reverses the direction of one WCS axis. The other two axes do not move.\n\nOpen design points:\n- A flip of one axis makes a left-handed frame. A rotation quaternion cannot store it. The WCS needs a sign for each axis, or an equivalent record, in the project file.\n- A flip changes the sign of posted coordinates on that axis. It also changes arc direction (`G2`/`G3`), cutter compensation side (`G41`/`G42`) and the climb or conventional sense. The post must write these correctly, or refuse the program with a named reason.\n- Simulation and the gouge checks must use the same frame as the post.\n- The gizmo must show the flipped axis.\n\nEach flip is an undoable command. It ships with an MCP tool, or a follow-up `MCP:` issue names it.\n\nEffort is `effort:high` because the change can alter posted G-code (safety floor).\n\nDone means: the three buttons are on the WCS page, each flip is one undo step, the project file keeps the flips, and golden NC tests pin the posted output of a flipped axis, with arcs and cutter compensation.\n",
